        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 10,
        "titulo": "Saying your job: no article, and the feminine in -in",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: with «sein» (and «werden»), the job comes WITHOUT an article: «Ich bin Lehrer», not «Ich bin ein Lehrer» — even though English says \"I'm a teacher\". STEP 2: the feminine usually adds -in to the masculine: Lehrer → Lehrerin, Student → Studentin, Kellner → Kellnerin. English mostly uses one word for both (\"teacher\", \"doctor\"); German almost always marks a woman's job with -in. STEP 3: some words also take an umlaut: Arzt → Ärztin, Koch → Köchin. STEP 4: questions: «Was sind Sie von Beruf?» (formal), «Was bist du von Beruf?» (informal) or «Was machst du beruflich?» — \"What do you do (for a living)?\". STEP 5: another way to say it: «Ich arbeite als Verkäufer» (I work as a salesperson), also without an article — just like \"work as\". Careful: «Krankenschwester» (female nurse) has a different masculine: «Krankenpfleger».",
        "comparacao": "The English reflex \"I'm a teacher\" produces «Ich bin ein Lehrer», which sounds wrong. German drops the article before a job (unless there's an adjective: «Er ist ein guter Lehrer»). And where English has gender-neutral job titles, German usually has two forms: Lehrer / Lehrerin.",
